<Base
  title="Cortex privacy policy · Colin Cheung"
  description="What Cortex does with Google account data."
>
  <article class="doc">
    <h1>Cortex privacy policy</h1>
    <p>Last updated 18 October 2026.</p>
    <p>
      Cortex is a personal tool that runs on my own PC, for my use only. The
      only Google account it signs in to is mine.
    </p>
    <h2>What it reads</h2>
    <p>
      With read-only access, it reads my Gmail and Google Calendar so it can
      tell whether a plan is already in my calendar.
    </p>
    <h2>What it writes</h2>
    <p>
      With calendar access, it adds, changes or removes an event in my Google
      Calendar only when I click to approve it, and it only changes or removes
      events it created itself. It never sends email.
    </p>
    <h2>Where the data goes</h2>
    <p>
      Data read from Google is processed and stored on my PC. It is not sold,
      shared with anyone or used for advertising.
    </p>
    <!-- prettier-ignore -->
    <p>
      Cortex's use of information received from Google APIs adheres to the
      <a href="https://developers.google.com/terms/api-services-user-data-policy">Google API Services User Data Policy</a>,
      including the Limited Use requirements.
    </p>
    <h2>Removing access</h2>
    <!-- prettier-ignore -->
    <p>
      Access can be withdrawn at any time at
      <a href="https://myaccount.google.com/connections">myaccount.google.com/connections</a>.
    </p>
    <h2>Contact</h2>
    <p>
      <a href="mailto:colincheung25@gmail.com">colincheung25@gmail.com</a>
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>

